    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "net",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 1: a flow network is a directed graph with a source s, a sink t, and a capacity on each edge."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 1 of 8: flow networks"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "stages"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "fd"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "fd[0..=1]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "A flow puts an amount on every edge. Two rules. First, never more than the capacity, and never negative."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "fd[2]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Second, conservation: at every vertex other than s and t, what flows in must flow out. Nothing is stored or lost on the way."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "fd[3]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "The value of a flow is the net amount leaving s, which equals the net amount reaching t. We want the largest value possible."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "fd[4..=5]"
      },
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "takeaway",
       "text": "respect capacities; conserve at every inner vertex"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "res",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 2: the key idea is the residual network: for every pair of vertices, how much more could we still send from u to v?"
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 2 of 8: residual network"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "fd"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "rd"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "If u to v is an edge, the answer is its unused capacity: capacity minus flow."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "rd[0]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "If v to u is an edge carrying flow, we may also send back from u to v, up to that flow: that simply cancels some of what we sent before."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "rd[1]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "The residual network keeps every pair with positive residual capacity. A simple path from s to t in it is called an augmenting path."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "rd[2..=3]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Its bottleneck, the smallest residual capacity on it, is how much more flow that path can carry."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "rd[4]"
      },
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "takeaway",
       "text": "augmenting path = s ⇝ t in G_f"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "cancel",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 3: why backward edges matter. A small network: every edge has capacity 2. Left: the flow, written flow over capacity. Right: the residual network."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
